1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,095
A seller on Instagram has no checkout: how do they take a card?

2
00:00:04,095 --> 00:00:09,654
With a payment link: the merchant's user makes it in the portal, and sends it to the buyer in any chat.

3
00:00:09,654 --> 00:00:15,726
Behind the link is an ordinary payment, with the same bank page, the same code, and the same debit.

4
00:00:15,726 --> 00:00:22,142
It has no API client and no return address, and its order number is made up, starting with link.

5
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:28,095
Only the owner makes one, and only for a live website: anything else gets the same refusal.

6
00:00:28,095 --> 00:00:33,488
The merchant chooses how long it works, up to the bank's configured limit, seven days.

7
00:00:33,488 --> 00:00:40,761
The link carries a random token, shown once, and the bank keeps only its hash, so a leaked table opens no page.

8
00:00:40,761 --> 00:00:46,892
The buyer opens it, enters the card and the code, and the page says paid, with no shop to go back to.

9
00:00:46,892 --> 00:00:52,809
Pages nobody pays in time, links and checkouts alike, are closed by a sweep every minute.

10
00:00:52,809 --> 00:00:59,321
It locks expired rows with skip locked, so each one closes once, however many instances run it.

11
00:00:59,321 --> 00:01:06,226
Expired is final, and like every ending it raises an event, so a website with a webhook hears about it.

12
00:01:06,226 --> 00:01:12,214
In the portal, the merchant sees each link's status: waiting, paid, or expired.

13
00:01:12,214 --> 00:01:15,869
Next, sending money back: refunds and reversals.

